{
  "_doc": "The town's wall clock (#789, #1030): a regulator in the pattern Connecticut makers sold to banks, depots, stores and saloons through the 1870s-80s (Seth Thomas's Regulator No. 2, Ansonia's and Waterbury's long-drop regulators): a walnut case hung on the wall, about 36 in high, a 12 in painted dial behind the upper glass of a full-length door, the brass-faced bob swinging behind the lower glass with REGULATOR in gilt on it, a moulded cornice and a sawn crest on top, a moulded base and a scrolled drop under it. Time only, eight-day, one winding hole. The maker's name on the dial and the label is invented (no real trade mark): \"H. ASHBY & SONS, BRISTOL, CONN.\". Hung on two nails through its back (KitProp `hangs`): it falls when its wall board goes. Its glass breaks as a window's; a ball through its works (`clock.works`) stops it at that minute (#789: a clue for the mission cards). ~6.5 kg. Written by tools/props/wall_clock.py.",
